icorecomp @ICORECOMP_PACKAGE_PLATFORM@
======================================

A native PC port of ICO (PlayStation 2, retail US), produced by static
recompilation. No game data ships with this build. You supply your own
disc image, dumped from your own copy.


Getting it running
------------------

1. Put your ICO (USA) disc image in this folder, named ico.iso.
   A raw bin/cue dump works too: name it ico.bin. The .cue file is not
   needed.

2. Run @ICORECOMP_PACKAGE_EXE@.

That is the whole install. Nothing is written outside this folder, and
nothing needs to be configured first.

If you would rather keep the image somewhere else, pass it on the command
line instead of step 1:

    @ICORECOMP_PACKAGE_EXE@ --disc "path/to/Ico (USA).iso"

The image is checked against the SHA-1 of the retail US boot ELF. A
mismatch is reported and the run stops, rather than failing strangely
later.


What this folder holds
----------------------

    @ICORECOMP_PACKAGE_EXE@
@ICORECOMP_PACKAGE_LIBS@
    README.txt@ICORECOMP_PACKAGE_UI@

The two libraries are the renderer (paraLLEl-GS) and SDL3, which provides
the window, controller input and audio. Both must stay next to the
executable.@ICORECOMP_PACKAGE_UI_NOTE@

After the first run you will also see icorecomp.log, the last run's log,
and saves/mc0, the virtual memory card.


Controls
--------

Any SDL3-supported controller (Xbox, DualSense, DualShock 4) is picked up
automatically. On the keyboard:

    arrows          d-pad
    WASD / IJKL     left / right stick
    X C Z V         cross / circle / square / triangle
    Q E 1 3         L1 / R1 / L2 / R2
    Enter           start
    Backspace       select


When something goes wrong
-------------------------

Every run writes icorecomp.log in this folder, replacing the previous
one. It holds the full log for that run, including the renderer and the
Vulkan driver, and it survives the window closing. If the port fails,
that file says why on one of its last few lines. Send it along with any
bug report.

If this folder cannot be written (an install under Program Files, a
read-only share), the log goes to %LOCALAPPDATA%\icorecomp instead, and
then to the temp folder. Whichever it picks, its first line names the
file, and the console says which locations it tried and why each failed.

The log can also carry diagnostic channels, which are never printed to
the console. They are off by default because they cost frame time.
ICORECOMP_VERBOSE=geom turns on the graphics one: it checks every
primitive sent to the graphics chip and reports, once per field, the ones
projected behind the eye or stretched past the hardware guard band, along
with which VU1 microprogram built them. If the picture is wrong, those
lines say so in numbers.

Requirements: a GPU and driver with Vulkan 1.1 or newer. The renderer
names the device it selected in the log.


Environment variables
---------------------

    ICORECOMP_LOG=path        write the log somewhere else ("-" disables)
    ICORECOMP_VERBOSE=list    extra diagnostic channels, comma separated,
                              or "all". Off by default: "geom" re-reads
                              every packet sent to the graphics chip, so
                              it costs frame time and is only worth it
                              when diagnosing the picture.
    ICORECOMP_GS=dump         headless, no window
    ICORECOMP_MAX_VBLANKS=N   stop after N fields
    ICORECOMP_SAVES_DIR=path  memory card location
    ICORECOMP_WAV_CAPTURE=out.wav
                              record the audio mix


License
-------

The port is MIT. It loads @ICORECOMP_PACKAGE_GSLIB@, which is paraLLEl-GS
under LGPLv3+, and SDL3 under the zlib license, both as separate shared
libraries. Source and the corresponding build instructions:
https://github.com/nathanialf/ico-recomp
